;; spec:   stdlib §4.14 (row println), §2.10 (the function twins), syntax §4.5 (println writes its text and a newline)
;; expect: accept
;; result: 0
;; audit:  clean
;; roots:  ../../lib support
;; covers: println run!
;; The twin of println, the one a program holds when it passes `println` as a
;; value, writes the text of its argument and then a newline. A case's result
;; is not what it writes, with one exception that this case uses: the compiled
;; runner reads the result from the last line of standard output, so a twin
;; that left its newline off would run its text into the result ("x0") and
;; fail under `fibc cases` where the interpreter's runner, which does not read
;; the output, cannot see it. The write is the last one of the program, and
;; the twin is reached through run!, not as the macro. Case 623 is the same
;; for prn's twin.
(ns main (:use fib.core fib.seq fib.print))

(defun main () -> i64
  (do (run! println ["x"])
      0))
